WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:03.290
John Talburt: Thank you.

2
00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:10.580
John Talburt: All right, well, welcome, everybody. This is our course on Principles of information quality.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.350
John Talburt: It's generally a gateway to our program, and so, for that reason, what I like to use, do

4
00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.989
John Talburt: is talk about the overall information quality program in general.

5
00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:35.950
John Talburt: And after that, I'll talk about the policies for this particular course, and if we have time, I may get started with some of the content. But mainly today, I wanted to kind of give an overview

6
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:42.550
John Talburt: So, of the graduate program, and some of you are in the master's, some of you are in the PhD.

7
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.840
John Talburt: Anyway, I think this might be helpful for you.

8
00:00:50.260 --> 00:01:03.470
John Talburt: So, first of all, I want to start off, that we all kind of agree, or, well, you don't have to agree, but this is the way we define information quality, and of course, there's a lot of ways to do it.

9
00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.409
John Talburt: Most people, when you talk about palsy, they just say accuracy.

10
00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.710
John Talburt: Another common one is Fitness for Use.

11
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.099
John Talburt: Others say, well, it's accurate, complete, and they start giving a lot of characteristics.

12
00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:20.730
John Talburt: Right information, right time, right place,

13
00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:26.099
John Talburt: More of a… a practice type of definition.

14
00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:36.949
John Talburt: And really, underneath, this one is pretty accurate. It's the application of total quality management Principles to building information products.

15
00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:43.830
John Talburt: It's not the formal definition, but this is where information quality as a discipline came from.

16
00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.520
John Talburt: So, back in the 1980s,

17
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:53.520
John Talburt: There was a movement in manufacturing called Total Quality Management.

18
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:56.579
John Talburt: And it really started in Japan.

19
00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:13.300
John Talburt: As you may… well, I'm sure you don't remember, but you may know that, after World War II, after the United States destroyed all of Japan's manufacturing, they got a chance to start all over again, and we helped them.

20
00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:17.579
John Talburt: And part of that help was that some of the people here in the U.S.

21
00:02:18.020 --> 00:02:24.220
John Talburt: who were advocates of quality, went over there to help the Japanese.

22
00:02:24.470 --> 00:02:28.249
John Talburt: Deming was one of them, a very important one.

23
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.929
John Talburt: And, Deming had many principles about

24
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:37.100
John Talburt: How you, you create a quality product.

25
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.740
John Talburt: And,

26
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:52.950
John Talburt: Anyway, the Japanese were very excited about it, and they adopted it, and as you may know also, they adopted it so well that they almost took over the car market. They actually did

27
00:02:53.110 --> 00:03:02.879
John Talburt: take away a huge chunk of the U.S. car market, because they manufactured automobiles, even today, like Toyotas and Hondas.

28
00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.370
John Talburt: That, were super high quality, reliable, dependable.

29
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.770
John Talburt: And, and so then it came back to the U.S, and

30
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.740
John Talburt: And of course, now just about everybody

31
00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:22.100
John Talburt: in manufacturing follows total quality management Principles. As a matter of fact.

32
00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:29.449
John Talburt: The International Organization for Standardization, called ISO, has,

33
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.559
John Talburt: Enshrine the total quality management in some

34
00:03:33.860 --> 00:03:45.110
John Talburt: standards, the ISO 9000 standards, and we'll talk more about that later. But it… but, anyway, so what happened is that,

35
00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.729
John Talburt: Before I get into our definition and finish this thought here.

36
00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:57.339
John Talburt: Is that, early on in the later 1980s and early 1990s.

37
00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:01.709
John Talburt: The people who were in the data management industry

38
00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.969
John Talburt: And especially in the academic side at MIT,

39
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:20.110
John Talburt: They saw this, and they said, you know, the way to think about data and how we're using data is that we're really manufacturing something. We're manufacturing a data or information product

40
00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:25.770
John Talburt: We're taking raw data, processing it through, and we're not using

41
00:04:25.970 --> 00:04:29.460
John Talburt: Mechanical machines, but we're using computers.

42
00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:36.609
John Talburt: We're using these computers to manipulate the raw data into an information product.

43
00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:43.630
John Talburt: We take data and make it into information by adding context around it, bringing things together.

44
00:04:43.680 --> 00:05:02.879
John Talburt: And so, by taking that idea that in data management, we're really manufacturing information products, and that we should manage information as a product, then why reinvent the wheel? Let's take the principles that have already been developed.

45
00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:06.919
John Talburt: In manufacturing, and apply them to data.

46
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.970
John Talburt: And so, this is… we have the discipline of… Information quality has,

47
00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:19.089
John Talburt: has evolved, and it's the reason, then, that I like to make a

48
00:05:19.270 --> 00:05:23.210
John Talburt: A distinction between information quality and data quality.

49
00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.330
John Talburt: Now, not everybody does.

50
00:05:25.930 --> 00:05:28.909
John Talburt: But since I'm the teacher, then I can do whatever I want to.

51
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:35.360
John Talburt: So, so, information quality is an information science discipline.

52
00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:46.819
John Talburt: And of course, hopefully by now you understand the difference between information science and computer science. Again, it's the same kind of two sides of the coin.

53
00:05:47.140 --> 00:06:06.430
John Talburt: computer science is about the computers and the hardware and the software, and the information science is about the data and the information. And of course, they work together. So, as a matter of fact, I don't know if you're aware or not, but there has been a change in alignment here at the university.

54
00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:13.239
John Talburt: And we no longer have a Department of Computer Science and a Department of Information Science.

55
00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.259
John Talburt: As of this summer, As of July the 1st.

56
00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:34.979
John Talburt: These two departments were merged together into a school, so we now have the School of Computer and Information Sciences. And of course, this program is a part of that school, so we have a new school here on campus.

57
00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:38.050
John Talburt: the School of Computer and Information Sciences.

58
00:06:38.540 --> 00:06:44.490
John Talburt: So, information quality is one of the information sciences, so it's a discipline

59
00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:55.150
John Talburt: And it's concerned with two main things. The first one is maximizing the value and minimizing the risk of an organization's information assets.

60
00:06:55.510 --> 00:06:58.999
John Talburt: So again, in this idea of thinking of

61
00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.829
John Talburt: Of information quality, or information

62
00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:23.449
John Talburt: in a manufacturing sense, right? Information is an asset. It's what we use to create value, for the organization. Just think about here at the university, one of the information products that we manufacture is your transcript.

63
00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:40.359
John Talburt: So the transcript has many feeds into it from different classes that you take, the grades that you receive, and so forth. These are brought together, integrated together, and put together, and the product is this transcript.

64
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:49.770
John Talburt: It's very important. It helps you keep track of where you are, but more importantly, once you've finished all the requirements, which again, they go from your transcript.

65
00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:52.860
John Talburt: Then it becomes a document.

66
00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:55.920
John Talburt: Often now a digital document.

67
00:07:55.990 --> 00:08:10.210
John Talburt: That you, for example, you did the master's, and you have your transcript showing you were awarded the master's degree. This is typically what you give as proof to an employer that you've completed your master's degree.

68
00:08:10.210 --> 00:08:24.540
John Talburt: Or if you go on to, say, a PhD degree at another school, again, they're going to request and say, we need proof that you've already achieved your master's, and so the transcript would be the way that that is communicated.

69
00:08:24.660 --> 00:08:32.910
John Talburt: So that's… that's an information product. Similarly, in a hospital, the same sort of thing happens in your medical chart.

70
00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:39.510
John Talburt: You may have many encounters in this hospital, other hospitals, outpatient, inpatient.

71
00:08:39.559 --> 00:08:53.859
John Talburt: All these are gathered together, and it's very important that all these are collected accurately, and that your chart reflects everything that's happened to you, and not anything that didn't happen to you from some other patient.

72
00:08:54.270 --> 00:09:00.050
John Talburt: So, creating these information products Now,

73
00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:04.119
John Talburt: So, the organization that's creating here at the university

74
00:09:05.270 --> 00:09:11.610
John Talburt: We create your transcript, that's our information product that we create. You're the consumer of that.

75
00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:16.769
John Talburt: So, we want to maximize the value of our information.

76
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:35.930
John Talburt: At the university, and so this is one way we do it. We also do it by taking the enrollment data and analyzing it to see if we're doing a good job, if we can recruit students, how the students are doing, what's causing students to fall out of the classes and leave the university.

77
00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:48.110
John Talburt: So we… we do a lot of analysis of this data, and we create a lot of those information products out of that data. All kinds of analyses and reports about, student…

78
00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:51.480
John Talburt: Continuation through the, through the program.

79
00:09:51.740 --> 00:10:01.889
John Talburt: So that's important, and it creates value for the organization. It helps us maintain enrollments, or increase enrollments, prevent students from getting at risk.

80
00:10:02.290 --> 00:10:09.629
John Talburt: And so that's from the internal side. The people who are producing, or the organization that's producing these products.

81
00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.650
John Talburt: But the other side of the coin.

82
00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:15.249
John Talburt: Is that we want to produce information products

83
00:10:15.410 --> 00:10:18.970
John Talburt: That create value for the users of those products.

84
00:10:19.580 --> 00:10:24.120
John Talburt: And so, again, looking at the transcript idea.

85
00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:42.739
John Talburt: that we want it to be valuable to you, and it is valuable to you. It's your proof that you've completed all your requirements, you've received this degree from an accredited university, and of course, there are many other products that we produce for other consumers. For example, the university's required to report

86
00:10:42.770 --> 00:10:46.130
John Talburt: To the Arkansas Department of Education.

87
00:10:46.370 --> 00:11:04.820
John Talburt: And they want to know, you know, who are the students, how are they doing? What majors do they have, what's their progress, how many of them are completing their degrees, how many are falling out and not completing their degrees, and they use this information

88
00:11:04.990 --> 00:11:20.150
John Talburt: And it's valuable to them, but again, at the same time, it becomes valuable to the university, because it's those reports that we get reimbursement or get, funding from the state for our students that we,

89
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:21.530
John Talburt: Enroll.

90
00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:34.340
John Talburt: So, again, there's two sides of the coin in the use of information. The value to the owner or the organization that has that information that's creating these information products.

91
00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:40.310
John Talburt: And the other side is, we want it to create value for the consumers of those products.

92
00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:45.380
John Talburt: So, anyway, the key word in our definition is value.

93
00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.050
John Talburt: It's all about creating value from information.

94
00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:52.260
John Talburt: Now,

95
00:11:52.400 --> 00:12:04.160
John Talburt: We'll talk a little bit more about the difference between information quality and data quality as we go on. But this is our definition. So we call our program, an information quality program.

96
00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:13.629
John Talburt: It's an Information Quality Master of Science Information Quality track in the PhD program, and it's an Information Quality,

97
00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:15.560
John Talburt: A graduate certificate.

98
00:12:16.160 --> 00:12:18.849
John Talburt: And those are the 3 degrees that we offer.

99
00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:27.480
John Talburt: Now, not long ago, an organization did a certification of information quality.

100
00:12:27.630 --> 00:12:33.230
John Talburt: It was information… IQCP, Information Quality Certified Professional.

101
00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:37.370
John Talburt: And in order to do that, they had to go through quite a bit of a study.

102
00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:43.429
John Talburt: And part of the study resulted in what's called the Information Quality Knowledge and Practice.

103
00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:53.700
John Talburt: and so, they categorize the knowledge and practice into, 6, groups.

104
00:12:54.410 --> 00:13:04.800
John Talburt: And the first one, again, coincides with our definition, information quality, value, and business impact, or should say organizational impact, because

105
00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:21.770
John Talburt: It's not just businesses that use information, it's a lot of nonprofits, like hospitals and governmental organizations. Obviously, the federal government, state government, they use a lot of information and create a lot of information products.

106
00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:25.980
John Talburt: And the question is, what's the value and impact of that?

107
00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:33.100
John Talburt: And then another that's become extremely important in recent days, or recent years.

108
00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:37.240
John Talburt: Is this idea of information quality, strategy, and governance.

109
00:13:37.630 --> 00:13:48.049
John Talburt: Back in the day, we just thought of information as a commodity, data, something we would get and use. It was all about creating the programs.

110
00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:54.389
John Talburt: Back in the day, when I don't know, I forget the exact number, but it was, like, 70% of…

111
00:13:54.490 --> 00:13:59.169
John Talburt: Freshmen entering MIT were going into computer science and programming.

112
00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:01.020
John Talburt: Because it was all about

113
00:14:01.550 --> 00:14:09.350
John Talburt: Creating these clever processes that would create these products, but the emphasis was just on the data.

114
00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:17.239
John Talburt: I'm sorry, just on the processing, the computer programming part of it, and not really about the data itself. But now.

115
00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:24.259
John Talburt: That's not such a big deal because, as you're probably aware and may be doing right now.

116
00:14:24.770 --> 00:14:31.530
John Talburt: Computer programming is kind of falling by the wayside. Software engineering is being taken over by AI.

117
00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:36.620
John Talburt: So, it's pretty easy now to use, Claude or some of these other,

118
00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:41.739
John Talburt: Language models to create all kinds of programs for you.

119
00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:51.239
John Talburt: And so, the need for having a staff of computer programmers, very knowledgeable about different types of languages and so forth.

120
00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:54.340
John Talburt: It's not as distinctive, it's not…

121
00:14:54.650 --> 00:15:05.409
John Talburt: Back when you had companies and they could afford to hire a lot of programmers, they had a huge advantage over small companies. But that's really not true anymore.

122
00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.589
John Talburt: So what, what is the competitive advantage?

123
00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:19.320
John Talburt: that these companies have. It's not the programming anymore. It's not having data centers anymore, or maintaining their own

124
00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:27.719
John Talburt: equipment. They don't have to have raised floors and have people that, take care of their computers for them, because now we have cloud computing.

125
00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.900
John Talburt: So the thing that's left that helps

126
00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:38.380
John Talburt: Distinguish one organization from another, especially if they're in the same kind of business.

127
00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.519
John Talburt: Is the data that they have.

128
00:15:40.910 --> 00:15:44.259
John Talburt: And so, you need stat… strategies

129
00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:56.240
John Talburt: For what data do we need to acquire? What kind of information products do we need to build to be competitive? And how do we maintain control over that data? And that's the idea of data governance.

130
00:15:56.950 --> 00:16:11.540
John Talburt: So that's become very important in the last few years, this idea of strategy. Now most… matter of fact, all governmental agencies are required to have what's called a chief data officer.

131
00:16:12.650 --> 00:16:19.350
John Talburt: And one of the most important roles of a Chief Data Officer is to create a data strategy for the company.

132
00:16:19.850 --> 00:16:25.520
John Talburt: So, strategy and then governance, have become very important. Governance, again, because

133
00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:42.059
John Talburt: You want to maintain control of data, but then there are also a lot of regulations around data. For example, here at the university, we're governed by a law called FERPA, which has to do with educational data, so it governs

134
00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:51.529
John Talburt: What data that we can release, what data is considered protected, and so we have to be very careful about that, and meet all of those,

135
00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:55.449
John Talburt: Requirements. So, that's part of, governance.

136
00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:00.549
John Talburt: And then, information quality, environment, and Culture.

137
00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:04.109
John Talburt: This is very important because

138
00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:07.570
John Talburt: The culture of an organization determines

139
00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:11.049
John Talburt: How it does business, how it gets things done.

140
00:17:11.329 --> 00:17:21.299
John Talburt: And this has really become extremely important in the last few years with the emergence of AI. We've had many things over the years that have changed our…

141
00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:25.550
John Talburt: culture, from big data now into AI,

142
00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:29.039
John Talburt: And we need to think about things in a different way.

143
00:17:29.140 --> 00:17:38.850
John Talburt: So the idea of change in culture… matter of fact, one of the courses that we teach here is Project and Change Management.

144
00:17:39.340 --> 00:17:53.109
John Talburt: And in change management, we don't mean, like, change in releases of code. We're talking about change in culture. How do you change culture so that you keep up? And we look at some use cases, or…

145
00:17:53.440 --> 00:18:00.639
John Talburt: Not use cases, but we'll look at some examples of what's happened to those people that are not willing to change their culture.

146
00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:10.840
John Talburt: for example, Kodak, That dominated, literally dominated the world in photography, is now just a small company.

147
00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:16.160
John Talburt: And the reason that they lost their lead was that they just didn't,

148
00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:25.610
John Talburt: changed their ideas about how photography was going to be done. They always thought it was going to be done with film, that digital was just a fad.

149
00:18:25.790 --> 00:18:28.530
John Talburt: And so they failed to change and keep up.

150
00:18:28.770 --> 00:18:31.820
John Talburt: And create the environment necessary to do that.

151
00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:39.250
John Talburt: So you'll notice that when talking about information quality, knowledge, and practice in these six main, groups.

152
00:18:39.550 --> 00:18:43.840
John Talburt: Of knowledge and practice, that the first three of them here

153
00:18:45.090 --> 00:18:56.630
John Talburt: really don't have anything to do with about dirty data. It's not talking about whether data is timely, whether it's accurate. It's talking about the management of information.

154
00:18:56.910 --> 00:19:00.170
John Talburt: So, that's the reason that our program here

155
00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:06.949
John Talburt: Is really… could be over in the business school as well as here in… but we ended up in the…

156
00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:13.310
John Talburt: College of, Science, technology, engineering, and mathematics.

157
00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:17.030
John Talburt: Which, again, so we're sort of half and half.

158
00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:19.670
John Talburt: And many people that join our program

159
00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:25.259
John Talburt: Do it because of that, because we have a strong management component to our program.

160
00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:31.469
John Talburt: And it's not all just a technical program. But we do get into the technical part.

161
00:19:31.710 --> 00:19:38.580
John Talburt: So we'll talk about information quality measurement and improvement, right? You can't manage something that you can't measure.

162
00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:43.039
John Talburt: So, how do you measure the quality of information and data?

163
00:19:43.250 --> 00:19:44.889
John Talburt: And how do you improve it?

164
00:19:47.080 --> 00:19:59.250
John Talburt: And how do you sustain that palsy over time? You need programs to do that. It's not just something you do as a one-time project. You need to constantly, be aware of it.

165
00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:06.550
John Talburt: And then, another thing that many people don't think about is that

166
00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:15.559
John Talburt: The quality of your information and your information products often depends on the architecture that you've built. Your information architecture.

167
00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:28.679
John Talburt: And sometimes it's just not adequate to maintain the quality or perform the way that you want it to. So you do have to pay attention to information architectures, and these are constantly changing, right? So…

168
00:20:28.890 --> 00:20:33.699
John Talburt: We go from, Client server to,

169
00:20:33.830 --> 00:20:41.930
John Talburt: big data, and now we're in an AI, we're talking about Agentic architectures, so there are many things that happen.

170
00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:44.240
John Talburt: Now, as I mentioned a while ago.

171
00:20:44.630 --> 00:20:50.529
John Talburt: This is the IQ International that developed this, the six domains.

172
00:20:50.630 --> 00:20:52.789
John Talburt: I did quite a bit of work in doing it.

173
00:20:54.860 --> 00:21:01.700
John Talburt: This part is what we call data quality, as opposed to the overall idea of information quality.

